ng to be reading here and then  reading there i just want to see the  words and work out the meaning in the  actual text so what i get out of this is  audio and text there's two hours worth  the first half of which now is not uh  you know interesting enough or  challenging enough for me but the big  thing too was  that six or seven years ago when i  studied korean  and i had bought this book at that time  and i had my other sources which i  preferred to use but then i'd go here  and listen and i couldn't understand it  and now i understand it although i'm  missing a few words it's clear to me is  that an epiphany moment i don't know but  it's content  two hours all right when i get going  like with russian  i would be downloading  more than two hours a day  i didn't listen to it all  i've interviewed interviews from echo  must be each  interview is 30 or 40 minutes long i  would easily take down two or three  interviews and during my driving i'd be  listening to them but that's when i'm at  a stage where i don't have to read  everything that i listen to so  you know  yeah i mean you need exposure here  there's two hours of exposure two hours  of exposure is not very much you need  tens of hours hundreds of hours  many hundreds of hours of exposure  before you get anywhere so assemble is  nice but it's just  one source of content to me that's all  it is it's a source of content it's okay  lots of disjointed phrases it's not  terribly interesting sound quality is  good so i'm going to do that for the  next day or two the the other advantage  of assimil for me at this stage is that  some of the stuff i'm doing uh voice of  russia and so forth is difficult so i  plow my way through it i'm getting  better it's nice to go back and do easy  stuff so assimil fits the bill  um and there you have it and now it's uh  hopefully i'm gonna do a bit of reading  before i go to bed  bye for now  just a final note when i said phonetic  script i was referring to a romanization  of kor